   "subject": "High-dimensional array subset to matrix?",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "Say I have 3-dimensional data, like:<div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">my_data </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> array</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">rnorm</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2000</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\">c</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">100</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">4</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">5</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span></div></code></div></div><div><br></div><div>I&#39;m then iterating through this data in my model:</div><div><br></div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">data </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#008\">int</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\">lower</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> I</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#008\">int</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\">lower</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> J</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#008\">int</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\">lower</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> K</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br><br>  real x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">I</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">J</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">K</span><span style=\"color:#660\">];</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#660\">...</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>parameters </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  real b</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">I</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">J</span><span style=\"color:#660\">];</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#660\">...</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>model </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><font color=\"#666600\"><span style=\"color:#000\">  </span><span style=\"color:#660\">...</span></font><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><font color=\"#000000\"><span style=\"color:#000\">  </span></font><span style=\"color:#008\">for</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i </span><span style=\"color:#008\">in</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\">I</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>    y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> multi_normal</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">b</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">],</span><span style=\"color:#000\">sigma_y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#660\">...</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span></div></code></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>In R, I would expect <div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">b</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">%*%</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,,]</span></div></code></div><br> to give me the correct vector. In stan, if I understand correctly, this code won&#39;t work because x[i] is a two-dimensional array of reals, not a proper matrix. Can I coerce it to be a matrix? Alternately, could I make x a vector of matrixes?</div><div><br></div><div>Or should I just always flatten everything to two dimensions as most?</div><div><br></div><div>Thanks,</div><div>BW</div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "Evmx4RPL-RAJ",
   "from": "Bob Carpenter",
   "author": "8aa9b8cc06fdc99c",
   "date": "2012-09-11T19:47:42Z",
   "subject": "Re: [stan-users] High-dimensional array subset to matrix?",
   "parts": [
    [
     3,
     "\r<br>\r<br>On 9/11/12 2:07 PM, BillWalker wrote:\r<br>&gt; Say I have 3-dimensional data, like:\r<br>&gt; |\r<br>&gt; my_data &lt;- array(rnorm(2000),c(100,4,5))\r<br>&gt; |\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; I&#39;m then iterating through this data in my model:\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; |\r<br>&gt; data {\r<br>&gt;    int&lt;lower=1&gt; I;\r<br>&gt;    int&lt;lower=1&gt; J;\r<br>&gt;    int&lt;lower=1&gt; K;\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;    real x[I,J,K];\r<br>&gt;    ...\r<br>&gt; }\r<br>&gt; parameters {\r<br>&gt;    real b[I,J];\r<br>&gt;    ...\r<br>&gt; }\r<br>&gt; model {\r<br>&gt;    ...\r<br>&gt; for(i in 1:I) {\r<br>&gt;      y[i] ~ multi_normal(b[i] * x[i],sigma_y);\r<br>&gt;    }\r<br>&gt;    ...\r<br>&gt; }\r<br>&gt; |\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; In R, I would expect\r<br>&gt; |\r<br>&gt; b[i,] %*% x[i,,]\r<br>&gt; |\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; to give me the correct vector. In stan, if I understand correctly, this code won&#39;t work because x[i] is a\r<br>&gt; two-dimensional array of reals, not a proper matrix. Can I coerce it to be a matrix? Alternately, could I make x a\r<br>&gt; vector of matrixes?\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; Or should I just always flatten everything to two dimensions as most?\r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "The simplest relevant declarations would be:\r<br>\r<br>    row_vector[J] b[I];  // b is an I-array of J-row-vectors\r<br>\r<br>    matrix[J,K]   x[I];  // x is an I-array of (JxK)-matrices\r<br>\r<br>though you could also define b with the same effect as:\r<br>\r<br>    matrix[I,J] b;\r<br>\r<br>We followed MATLAB, not R/BUGS in our matrix operators,\r<br>so you can just use:\r<br>\r<br>    (b[i] * x[i])&#39;\r<br>\r<br>to produce a column vector (b[i] * x[i] is a row vector).\r<br>\r<br>We are going to generalize multi_normal in an upcoming\r<br>release to allow row vectors (as well as 1D arrays)\r<br>of matching dimension as well as vectors as arguments.\r<br>\r<br>You can also save a transpose by storing x in the opposite\r<br>order, with:\r<br>\r<br>    matrix[K,J] xt[I];  // xt[i] = x[i]&#39;\r<br>\r<br>    vector[J] bt[I];     //\r<br>\r<br>In this arrangement, (xt[i] * bt[i]) is a column vector\r<br>so you don&#39;t need the transpose.  (You could even define\r<br>xt as transformed data so you could use the original data\r<br>coding.)\r<br>\r<br>- Bob\r<br>\r<br>\r<br>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "thM5WlKm55oJ",
   "from": "Andrew Gelman",
   "author": "da33143a8db60ce8",
   "date": "2012-09-11T20:56:55Z",
   "subject": "Re: [stan-users] High-dimensional array subset to matrix?",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\">It might be a good idea in the manual to have an example like this, in which we format the same array in several different ways, so users can see the equivalencies.  We can then also explain why some parameterizations work more efficiently than others.<div>A</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\"><div><br><div><div>On Sep 11, 2012, at 2:07 PM, BillWalker wrote:</div><br><blockquote type=\"cite\">Say I have 3-dimensional data, like:<div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">my_data </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> array</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">rnorm</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2000</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\">c</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">100</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">4</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">5</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span></div></code></div></div><div><br></div><div>I&#39;m then iterating through this data in my model:</div><div><br></div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">data </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#008\">int</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\">lower</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> I</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#008\">int</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\">lower</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> J</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#008\">int</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\">lower</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> K</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br><br>  real x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">I</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">J</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">K</span><span style=\"color:#660\">];</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#660\">...</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>parameters </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  real b</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">I</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">J</span><span style=\"color:#660\">];</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#660\">...</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>model </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><font color=\"#666600\"><span style=\"color:#000\">  </span><span style=\"color:#660\">...</span></font><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><font color=\"#000000\"><span style=\"color:#000\">  </span></font><span style=\"color:#008\">for</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i </span><span style=\"color:#008\">in</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\">I</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>    y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> multi_normal</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">b</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">],</span><span style=\"color:#000\">sigma_y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#660\">...</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span></div></code></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>In R, I would expect <div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">b</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">%*%</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,,]</span></div></code></div><br> to give me the correct vector. In stan, if I understand correctly, this code won&#39;t work because x[i] is a two-dimensional array of reals, not a proper matrix. Can I coerce it to be a matrix? Alternately, could I make x a vector of matrixes?</div><div><br></div><div>Or should I just always flatten everything to two dimensions as most?</div><div><br></div><div>Thanks,</div><div>BW</div></blockquote></div><br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "D8cKGGzTD1sJ",
   "from": "Bob Carpenter",
